 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DetcEbosO-q/",
 "title": "Why Helping People Can Backfire",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that offering unsolicited help or teaching others often puts you in a superior position and can lead them to resent you. He advises waiting for people to ask for help before offering advice.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator claims 90% of people you give advice to will eventually turn their back on you or get angry at you.",
  "Teaching someone automatically puts you in a higher position, which can be dangerous because you do not know how they perceive you.",
  "The creator uses an analogy: someone is at the bottom of a hole, but to them, that is where they want to be or feel trapped.",
  "When you forcefully pull someone out of a hole, they may see you as the person who hurt them rather than the person who saved them.",
  "Stop trying to pull everyone out of their problems; let people stay where they choose to stay.",
  "Only give advice when they raise their hand, ask for help, and confirm they really want your advice.",
  "Remember that not everyone who needs your help actually wants your help."
 ],
 "content": "Helping People is the start of building hate. Helping people is the how they hate you and creating your own enemy. You give them advice because you really want to help them, right? But instead of thanking you, 90% of them will eventually turn their back on you and get angry at you. And the worst part, you think that you did nothing wrong. If you can understand this, you will know exactly when to speak and when to just shut up. We as a healthcare provider, especially when we become more senior, right? Naturally, we want to teach our junior, and we want to share our experience. Yes, part of it is because we really want to help. But just imagine another part of it is because we want to share. But you have to understand one thing, the moment you start teaching someone, you automatically put yourself into a higher position. That's where it become very dangerous, because you have no idea how the other person see you. Think about it like this. Someone is sitting at the bottom of the hole. From where you are standing, you think that, hey, why are you still down there? Let me pull you out. But they never raise up their hand, they never ask you to pull them up. Maybe to you they are trapped. To them, that's where they want to be. And when you forcefully, finally pull them out, they don't see you as the person who saved them. They see you as the person who hurt them. So stop trying to pull everyone out of their hole. Let people stay where they choose to stay. Only when they raise their hand and ask you for help, then at this moment you ask them again, do you really want my advice? When they say yes, then you reach your hand down, pull them up. Just remember, not everyone who needs your help wants your help. If you want to learn more about how to communicate and how to teach people, how to coach people, remember to follow me. \n\nOn screen text: Helping People is the start of building hate",
